  "prompt": "Which of Georgia O’Keeffe's works at the Art Institute of Chicago, as of June 2024, were landscapes of New Mexico created before 1940 with oil painting?",
  "prompt_prefix": "",
  "instruction": "Which of Georgia O’Keeffe's works at the Art Institute of Chicago, as of June 2024, were landscapes of New Mexico created before 1940 with oil painting?",
  "answer": "Based on my research of the Art Institute of Chicago's collection as of June 2024, **two** Georgia O'Keeffe works meet all your criteria (New Mexico landscape, created before 1940, oil painting):\n\n1. **Black Cross, New Mexico (1929)** — Oil on canvas. Depicts a Catholic cross against rolling hills and a sunset in the New Mexico landscape. Acquired by the Art Institute in 1943. Currently on view in Gallery 161.\n\n2. **Red Hills with Flowers (1937)** — Oil on canvas. Shows vibrant blossoms magnified against the dry, steep hills surrounding her New Mexico home. Currently off view.\n\n**Works excluded and why:**\n- *Cow's Skull with Calico Roses* (1931) — Oil on canvas, New Mexico-sourced subject, but primarily a still life of a skull with fabric roses, not a landscape\n- *The Black Place* (1943) and *The White Place in Sun* (1943) — New Mexico landscapes in oil, but created after 1940\n- *White Shell with Red* (1938) — New Mexico landscape but pastel, not oil\n- *Green Mountains, Canada* (1932), *Yellow Hickory Leaves with Daisy* (1928), *The Shelton with Sunspots, N.Y.* (1926), *Ballet Skirt* (1927), *Blue and Green Music* (1919–21) — Not New Mexico landscapes\n- *Road – Mesa with Mist* (1961), *Sky above Clouds IV* (1965) — Post-1940",
  "answer_chars": 1244,
  "expected_answer": "Red Hills with Flowers (1937), Black Cross, New Mexico (1929)",
